        <span>Rook Ceph RBD FailedMount Recovery</span>
      </div>

      <header class="case-detail-header">
        <span class="section-label">CASE STUDY · KUBERNETES · ROOK CEPH</span>

        <h1>
          Recovering a Kubernetes Pod blocked by an old RBD mapping
        </h1>

        <p class="case-lead">
          A Pod remained in <code>ContainerCreating</code> while CSI repeatedly
          reported that the RBD image was still being used. Scanning the RBD
          nodeplugins traced the failure to a mapping that remained on a node.
          Cleanup was performed only after confirming the old workload had
          stopped and checking the mount and device state.
        </p>

        <div class="case-facts">
          <div>
            <span>Environment</span>
            <strong>Kubernetes + Rook Ceph RBD</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span>Primary signal</span>
            <strong>FailedMount · image is still being used</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span>Diagnosis</span>
            <strong>Old RBD mapping remained on a CSI node</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span>Outcome</span>
            <strong>CSI remapped the volume; the Pod recovered</strong>
          </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <article class="case-detail-content">

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">01 · Problem</span>
          <h2>A Pod could not get past ContainerCreating</h2>

          <p>
            The Pod stayed in <code>ContainerCreating</code>, and
            <code>kubectl describe pod</code> repeatedly showed a mount failure:
          </p>

          <pre><code>Warning  FailedMount  kubelet
MountVolume.MountDevice failed for volume "pvc-xxxxxxxx":
rbd image ceph-blockpool/csi-vol-xxxxxxxx is still being used</code></pre>

          <p>
            This was a storage handoff problem, not something a Pod recreation
            alone could clear. The message also signaled that another client
            might still own the RBD image, so the next step was to find the
            mapping rather than force the new mount.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">02 · Evidence</span>
          <h2>Trace the claim from Kubernetes to the CSI node</h2>

          <p>
            The Pod, PVC, PV and attachment state established which volume was
            involved and where Kubernetes expected it to be attached:
          </p>

          <pre><code>kubectl describe pod -n &lt;namespace&gt; &lt;pod-name&gt;
kubectl get pvc -n &lt;namespace&gt; &lt;pvc-name&gt;
kubectl get pv &lt;pv-name&gt; -o yaml
kubectl get volumeattachment</code></pre>

          <p>
            The investigation then located the Rook RBD nodeplugins and checked
            their local mappings:
          </p>

          <pre><code>kubectl -n rook-ceph get pod -o wide | grep csi-rbdplugin
kubectl -n rook-ceph exec &lt;nodeplugin-pod&gt; -c csi-rbdplugin -- rbd device list</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">03 · Diagnosis</span>
          <h2>The target image was still mapped on a node</h2>

          <p>
            Scanning the RBD nodeplugins found the target image on an old node,
            represented here with an anonymized image and device:
          </p>

          <pre><code>ceph-blockpool/csi-vol-xxxxxxxx → /dev/rbd5</code></pre>

          <p>
            A clean result from <code>rbd device list</code> in
            <code>rook-ceph-tools</code> would only describe that Pod. It would
            not rule out a mapping on another Kubernetes node. The direct
            blocker was the old mapping; the available record does not establish
            why it had remained there.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">04 · Safety check</span>
          <h2>Confirm ownership before removing the mapping</h2>

          <p>
            “Still being used” was treated as a data-safety signal, not an
            instruction to force an unmap. Before cleanup, the old workload was
            confirmed stopped, there was no legitimate writer, and the mount
            state was checked on the nodeplugin holding the mapping.
          </p>

          <pre><code>mount | grep /dev/rbdX</code></pre>

          <p>
            Only after confirming that the volume was no longer in active use
            was it safe to remove the old mount and mapping. Force unmap,
            blocklisting and deleting a <code>VolumeAttachment</code> were not
            used as default recovery steps.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">05 · Action</span>
          <h2>Unmount first, then unmap the RBD image</h2>

          <p>
            The recorded recovery followed the normal cleanup order: release the
            remaining filesystem mount, then remove the stale RBD mapping.
          </p>

          <pre><code>umount &lt;mount-point&gt;
rbd unmap ceph-blockpool/csi-vol-xxxxxxxx</code></pre>

          <p>
            With the old mapping gone, CSI could perform the new mapping through
            its regular attach and mount flow.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">06 · Verification</span>
          <h2>CSI mapped the volume again and the Pod recovered</h2>

          <p>
            After the unmount and unmap, CSI could map the image again and the
            Pod recovered. The repeated <code>FailedMount</code> stopped
            blocking the mount operation.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a stateful workload, confirming that the Pod runs is not the
            same as validating application-level integrity. Application I/O
            should be checked separately; that check is not claimed as part of
            this incident record.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">07 · Outcome</span>
          <h2>A vague FailedMount became a specific storage finding</h2>

          <p>
            The investigation narrowed the failure to an old RBD mapping that
            remained on a node. After confirming the old workload was no longer
            a writer, the mount and mapping were cleaned up, CSI took over the
            volume again, and the Pod recovered.
          </p>

          <p>
            This confirms the direct failure point and recovery outcome; it does
            not establish the upstream reason the mapping was left behind.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">08 · Takeaway</span>
          <h2>Diagnose first. Change second.</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>
              “Image is still being used” is an ownership and data-safety signal,
              not just a generic Ceph error.
            </li>
            <li>
              Kubernetes attachment state, CSI node mappings and Ceph client
              state are different layers; inspect the layer holding the image.
            </li>
            <li>
              Confirm that the old writer has stopped before removing a mount or
              mapping.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

      </article>

